PANTHEON — The History Stereoscope

Version 1.0 · Humanity, civilization, living memory, and deep time

The objective is to reconstruct history from many perspectives simultaneously—not simply to learn what happened, but to understand how reality was experienced, interpreted, transmitted, forgotten, and transformed.

And we won’t stop at humanity. Other species have histories too. Some transmit learned behaviors, some transform their environments, and some leave physical records of conditions that existed before humans were there to witness them.

Human civilization: records, institutions, cultural inheritance

Other animals: behavior, social learning, local traditions

The architecture

A traditional historical account follows a timeline. Our stereoscope follows a network of observers moving through time, each with different access to reality.

Every historical observation will have seven coordinates.

Coordinate	What it captures
Time	When the event occurred, when it was remembered, and when it was interpreted
Place	Geographic location, movement, connections, and boundaries
Scale	Individual, family, class, community, civilization, planet
Horizon	What an observer could realistically know or experience
Perspective	Language, culture, incentives, identity, worldview, interpretation
Transmission	How knowledge moves, mutates, survives, or disappears
Evidence	What independently supports, contradicts, or remains unknown about an account

The key is that these coordinates can be changed independently. We can move backward 500 years without leaving a particular geographic location, or examine the same year from ten civilizations, or descend from an emperor’s interpretation to the experience of a single farmer.

And we keep three histories distinct:

Lived history: What participants encountered, understood, and did at the time.

Remembered history: What people, families, communities, and institutions subsequently preserved and passed down.

Reconstructed history: What later historians and scientists infer from the surviving evidence.

They interact, but they are not interchangeable.

The stereoscope should expose those differences rather than erase them.

