The atlas includes human origins, Achaemenid Persia, the Silk Roads, Polynesian wayfinding, medieval Mali, the Atlantic encounter, the Haitian Revolution, and Iran’s 1953 coup.

It also includes chimpanzee tool traditions, humpback whale songs, sperm whale vocal clans, tree rings, and coral archives. Studies of animal cultural transmission and environmental proxies provide scientific foundations for these comparisons.

One example: Iran, 1953

The stereoscope lets you compare an Iranian protester’s possible knowledge with that of a covert planner and a historian working decades afterward.

The historian can access formerly classified records that neither ordinary citizens nor many contemporaneous political participants could access at the time.

That reveals something our usual narratives frequently conceal: information inequality between historical actors can be as consequential as ideological disagreement.

And the instrument can examine that inequality at individual, community, civilizational, and planetary scales.

The next expansion: the living history of Earth

This is where I’d eventually take it.

Historical substrate	What survives
Human culture	Language, records, memories, institutions
Animal culture	Learned behaviors, calls, traditions
Genetic inheritance	Heritable biological information
Organismal structure	Growth patterns and environmental responses
Ecosystems	Changing relationships, populations and landscapes
Geological strata	Physical, chemical and fossil traces of deep time

These aren’t identical forms of memory. But they can all preserve information about earlier states through different mechanisms.

The larger Pantheon instrument could eventually connect them into a multiscale history of life, following not only human civilizations but the relationships between evolving organisms, their environments, their inherited information, and their transformations.

And perhaps its most important capability will be moving between two views:

The world as its inhabitants could understand it then.

The world as we can reconstruct it now.

Between those two views lie the gaps, surprises, misinterpretations, hidden dependencies, and discoveries we’ve been discussing.

For the repository, the files are self-contained. You can place the HTML at docs/history-stereoscope.html and the semantic seed at docs/HISTORY_STEREOSCOPE_SEED.md. No ZIP, framework, or build system is necessary.

We’ve built the first instrument. The larger historical atlas remains to be populated, cross-checked, and expanded—without pretending that a handful of perspectives can exhaust humanity’s history.

The territory is bigger than any map. Now we have a way to compare the maps.